Description: GtkGLArea: the format and the color state of the picture
 A GtkGLArea rendered into 8 bits a channel, and its picture was taken as
 sRGB. gtk_gl_area_set_format() takes a GdkMemoryFormat: 8 bits (the default),
 10 bits (ABGR2101010), 16 bits (R16G16B16A16, which also carries pictures of
 12 bits: there is no format of 12), half float and single float.
 gtk_gl_area_set_color_state() takes the GdkColorState the values are in
 (sRGB by default; Rec. 2100 PQ, Rec. 2100 linear, sRGB linear, or any state
 built from CICP numbers such as hybrid log-gamma). The framebuffer of the
 render signal, the texture GTK draws and the dma-buf handed on have that
 format and that color state, per view in a stereo area.
 The picture of a stereo area is opaque: in 8 and 10 bits its dma-buf format
 says so (XB24, XB30); GDK knows no opaque form of the 16 bit and half float
 formats, so a deeper picture keeps its format (AB48, AB4H) and its alpha is
 made one. Half float is the deepest float format a dma-buf has, so the views
 of a single float area are rendered in single floats and their picture is
 kept in half floats. Where the driver exports no dma-buf of the format, or
 GDK cannot build the texture, the area says why in stereo-error and draws
 its left view alone; a failure found while the area is being drawn draws it
 again at once. On X11 a stereo area with a color state other than sRGB says
 that it cannot be shown per eye: an X window has no color description.
 Also here, as later corrections of the area: on X11 the way to the child
 window follows the renderer (OpenGL on a graphics card, the readback on a
 software renderer, by the processor each costs; GDK_X11_STEREO_READBACK=1 or
 =0 chooses), and testsuite/gtk/glarea gains the tests of the new properties
 and skips its stereo-unavailable case on a desktop whose compositor takes
 stereo content.
